jobs.description:
**This position is open to candidates with existing work authorization in the location where the position is advertised.**

**Application deadline:** Applications are accepted on an ongoing basis.

**Background/IRC Summary:**

The International Rescue Committee's (IRC) External Relations (ER) department enables the organization to respond to the world’s worst humanitarian crises and helps people to survive and rebuild their lives. Within ER, the Philanthropy team leads the organization’s work with High-Net-Worth Individuals. We are seeking an ambitious Principal Giving East Manager to join our USA Philanthropy team!

**Job Overview/Summary:**

The Manager, Principal Giving East will play a meaningful role in supporting the Principal Giving East team within the Philanthropy team. They will help guide distinct portfolios of high-net-worth donors and prospects and will act in very close collaboration with Principal Giving Officers and leadership to track progress on and implement strategy for some of our most valuable donor relationships.

The Manager will work with assigned Principal Giving Officer(s) to support the cultivation, solicitation and stewardship for a defined portfolio of IRC prospects and donors. The Manager will monitor and coordinate stewardship reporting requirements, ongoing communication and information management for the Officer's portfolio and will be responsible for updating all activity in donor database. The Manager will also collaborate across the External Relations team to coordinate donor engagement, stewardship, and recognition consistent with donor stewardship plans, and to maintain up-to-date portfolios for assigned Principal Giving Officer(s). The Principal Giving Manager will report to the Associate Director of Principal Giving.

This role offers a dynamic growth opportunity for someone eager to advance to front-line fundraising.

**Major Responsibilities:**

- Collaborate with assigned Principal Giving Officer(s) to implement strategies for high-net-worth individual donors and prospects within donor portfolios, bringing in critical revenue for the organization;
- Draft correspondence that support the Officer’s interactions with portfolio donors or prospects, including general and targeted communications such as letters and contact reports.
- Coordinate with relevant internal teams to develop communications that support donor strategy such as concept notes, proposals, presentations and reports, from drafts through to final line-editing and formatting of finalized material;
- Prepare materials, agendas, and briefings for external prospect and donor meetings, and for internal strategy meetings;
- With the Officer(s), assess expectations and objectives for each donor and prospect within the portfolio, including adhering to stewardship plans and strategies;
- With the Officer(s), act as the liaison between donors or prospects and field staff, ensuring both parties receive the most critical information and that it is communicated clearly and strategically reflective of its audience;
- Collaborate closely with Prospect Research and Management teams as well as Analytics and operations staff to co-lead and track the entire donor cycle for Principal Giving.
- Update the donor database (Salesforce) to maintain portfolio assignments, draft and update giving proposals, request prospect research, monitor report requirements and submissions, track ongoing donor communication and activity as well as additional relationship management duties;
- Manage information flows between donors and prospects, program staff and Officer(s);
- Lead and track the internal drafting and approval processes necessary for proposals, reports, and budgets;
- Support the payments for bank transactions. Ensure completeness of payment documents in compliance with IRC and Donor policies and procedures.
- Develop and maintain excellent working relationships with colleagues and IRC program/support staff globally, and
- Undertake special projects as advised.

**Job Requirements:**

**Work Experience:** Minimum 3 years previous work experience, preferably gained in a development or fundraising team, non-profit, marketing, or sales team.

**Proven Skills and Proficiencies:**

**Minimum:**

- Ability to meet deadlines with quality products.
- Strong communications skills including oral, written and presentation skills.
- Well-developed project management skills with the ability to work on own initiative, accurately to tight deadlines, and to prioritize between conflicting demands;
- Understanding of the role of written communications in building and maintaining sophisticated individual and institutional relationships;
- Ability to identify, create, and implement processes necessary to support effective donor relationships;
- Experience with and knowledge of relevant technologies and tools. with excellent analytical skills combined with the substantiated ability to multi-task, prioritize duties, and handle time effectively.
- Ability to collaborate across teams internally, regardless of distance, and garner support for and complete initiatives effectively;
- Highly computer literate, to include Word, Excel, PowerPoint as well as familiarity with CRM databases, including Salesforce;
- Ability to respond quickly to changes in short-term work priorities;
- Ability to analyze and interpret financial data;
- Dedication to fundraising for international development, humanitarian, human rights, social justice and US immigrant communities;
- Understanding of the US fundraising marketplace.

**Working Environment:** This role requires working remotely part-time (i.e., telecommuting). Applicants must have a home or alternate workspace they can effectively complete their work from during regularly scheduled hours.

**Compensation:** (Pay Rate: $67,000-$80,000/yr). Ranges are based on various factors including the labor market, job type, internal equity, and budget. Exact offers are calibrated by work location, individual candidate experience and skills relative to the defined job requirements.

**Equal Opportunity Employer:** IRC is an Equal Opportunity Employer. IRC considers all applicants on the basis of merit without regard to race, sex, color, national origin, religion, sexual orientation, age, marital status, veteran status, disability or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.

**Professional Standards:** All International Rescue Committee workers must adhere to the core values and principles outlined in IRC Way - Standards for Professional Conduct. Our Standards are Integrity, Service, Equality and Accountability. In accordance with these values, the IRC operates and enforces policies on Safeguarding, Conflicts of Interest, Fiscal Integrity, and Reporting Wrongdoing and Protection from Retaliation. IRC is committed to take all necessary preventive measures and create an environment where people feel safe, and to take all necessary actions and corrective measures when harm occurs. IRC builds teams of professionals who promote critical reflection, power sharing, debate, and objectivity to deliver the best possible services to our clients.

**Current Benefits:** IRC offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package. Highlights include 10 paid sick leave, 10 U.S. holidays, 20-25 generous paid time off based on role and tenure, medical insurance starting at $163/month, dental starting at $6.50/month, and vision starting at $5/month, flexible spending accounts (healthcare, dependent care, and commuter), a 403(b) retirement savings plan with employer match (immediate vesting) along with a non-elective employer contribution based on age and years of service (3-year vesting), disability and life insurance, and an Employee Assistance Program (EAP) offering confidential counseling and support services for employees and their families.

**US Benefits effective Jan 1, 2027:** IRC offers a comprehensive and highly competitive set of benefits. In the US, these include: 10 sick days, 10 US holidays, 20-30 paid time off days depending on role and tenure, medical insurance starting at $187 per month, dental starting at $7.70 per month, and vision starting at $5 per month, FSA for healthcare, dependent care, and commuter costs, a 403b retirement savings plans with immediately vested matching, disability & life insurance, and an Employee Assistance Program which is available to our staff and their families to support counseling and care in times of crisis and mental health struggles.

## Mission description

Under the management of the project manager, and in connection with the headquarters teams within the PRAR thematic division, the Thematic Manager ensures the coherence and technical implementation of the project's objectives (risk management and civil protection).

He/She guarantees the relevance, coherence, and quality of the technical activities implemented, in accordance with the project objectives and the guidelines of the Project Manager. He/She ensures the coordination of the different project components and coordinates and manages the technical contributions of the implementing partners.

Based in Amman, he/she acts as the content coordinator, supervises the experts (both permanent and short-term), and guides the technical strategy around the theme of Prevention, Preparedness, and Response to Disasters in the region.

Its missions are as follows:

**1. Programming and technical strategy**

- Define the technical guidelines for the entire project (pillars 1 and 2) in line with the project objectives (logical framework) and the guidelines of the Project Steering Committee, ensuring their consistency with international and regional strategic frameworks, notably the Sendai Framework;
- Define and propose to the Project Manager activities that allow these technical directions to be operationally implemented;
- Develop and update the technical programming, ensuring the relevance, feasibility, and complementarity of activities;
- Ensure coherence and proper coordination between the two pillars of the project (regional cooperation pillar and CRAFS pillar).

**2. Supervision of expertise and technical quality assurance**

- Ensure functional coordination and technical supervision of project experts, especially those integrated into the project team;
- Develop terms of reference for activities and specific technical expertise;
- Coordinate the technical contributions of consortium members, promoting complementarity of expertise and harmonization of methodological approaches;
- Supervise the development of intervention methodologies, work plans, and technical content for activities, in collaboration with experts and implementing partners;
- Ensure quality control of activities and technical deliverables (reports, training, methodological materials, etc.), ensuring their relevance and suitability to the needs of beneficiaries;
- Identify technical difficulties likely to affect the implementation of activities or the ownership of results and propose corrective measures

**3. Technical dialogue, partnerships, and governance support**

- Contribute to the project start-up phase (inception phase) by refining the needs analysis, specifying intervention priorities, and contributing to the consolidation of the work plan;
- Develop and maintain working relationships with national civil protection authorities and other institutional stakeholders, technical and scientific institutions, regional organizations (UfM, UNDRR, League of Arab States...), and other project stakeholders;
- Participate in technical exchanges with DG ECHO, consortium members, and beneficiary institutions, and ensure, when necessary, the technical representation of the project;
- Organize and lead technical coordination meetings, contribute to the work of governance bodies, and participate in steering committees (COPIL);
- Provide the Project Manager with the technical analyses and recommendations necessary for decision-making;
- Mapping, and if relevant, promoting, synergies with other projects and cooperation initiatives in civil protection and disaster risk management, notably those funded by the European Union or implemented by Expertise France;
- Contribute to the preparation of technical reports, concept notes, strategic documents, and project presentation materials.

**4. Monitoring results and sustainability**

- Support the Project Monitoring – Evaluation function, including defining relevant and achievable indicators (logical framework);
- Ensure the integration of feedback and lessons learned from activities into the continuous improvement of project approaches and methodologies;
- Contribute to capitalization processes, sharing of best practices, and transfer of skills to partner institutions;
- Favoriser le développement de réseaux d'expertise et de communautés de pratiques entre les institutions des pays partenaires et leurs homologues européens, afin de renforcer les échanges d'expériences et la coopération technique régionale ;
- Veiller à la pérennisation des acquis du projet au-delà de sa mise en œuvre (enjeu de soutenabilité et appropriation) et au renforcement durable des capacités institutionnelles et opérationnelles des pays partenaires.

## Description du projet ou contexte

Le programme européen *Prevention, Preparedness and Response to Natural and Man-Made Disasters in the Mediterranean* (PPRD Med) s’inscrit dans une coopération de longue date entre l’Union européenne et les pays de la rive Sud Méditerranée en matière de protection civile et de gestion des risques de catastrophe. Ses phases successives ont contribué au renforcement des capacités nationales de prévention, de préparation et de réponse, ainsi qu’au développement de la coopération régionale.

Dans la continuité des précédentes phases du programme, le projet PPRD-Med II ambitionne de renforcer la dimension opérationnelle de cette coopération, notamment à travers le développement des capacités de gestion des risques, la promotion de l'interopérabilité, la mobilisation de nouvelles technologies et le partage d'expertise entre les acteurs euro-méditerranéens.

Le projet envisagé s'articule autour de deux composantes complémentaires :

- **Pilier 1** : Une composante régionale de coopération en matière de protection civile, qui vise à renforcer les capacités régionales de prévention, de préparation et de réponse aux catastrophes, tout en favorisant le rapprochement avec le mécanisme de protection civile de l’Union européenne (UCPM).
- **Pilier 2 :** Une composante dédiée à l'opérationnalisation du Cyprus Regional Aerial Firefighting Station (CRAFS), situé à Paphos, à Chypre, afin de contribuer au développement des capacités régionales de lutte contre les feux de forêt, de formation et de coopération opérationnelle.

Le projet sera mis en œuvre par Expertise France, en qualité de chef de file, en partenariat avec un consortium d'institutions et d'organisations techniques européennes et méditerranéennes, et en étroite collaboration avec la DG ECHO, l'Union pour la Méditerranée (UpM) et les autorités nationales des pays partenaires.

Les pays bénéficiaires du projet sont les suivants : Algérie, Égypte, Israël, Jordanie, Liban, Libye, Maroc, Mauritanie, Palestine, Syrie, Tunisie.

Pour assurer la mise en œuvre de PPRD-Med II, Expertise France recrute une équipe dédiée (« Equipe projet ») composée :

- D’un.e Chef.fe de projet (poste temps plein) ;
- D’un.e Responsable technique (poste temps plein) ;
- D’un.e Chargé.e d’appui aux opérations (poste temps plein) ;
- D’un.e Expert.e Gestion des risques de catastrophe - Prévention (consultant.e, temps partiel) ;
- D’un.e Expert.e Protection Civile – Préparation & Réponse (consultant.e, temps partiel).

The project team will be based within the Expertise France office in Jordan, which will serve as the project's permanent office. It will benefit from the logistical, administrative, and financial support of the office's "Project Support Unit – USP," notably for procurement procedures, contracting, payment processing, human resources management, and logistics of missions and activities within Jordanian territory. .

Additionally, the project will benefit from the support of the Expertise France headquarters, notably the thematic expertise of the PRAR Division (Prevention, Reduction, and Adaptation to Risks)

## Desired profile

**1. Expertise and required skills**
Recognized expertise in the following areas:
• Disaster Management (GRC) / Civil Protection cycle;
• Specific expertise in forest fire risk management;
• Excellent understanding of the European Union Civil Protection Mechanism (UCPM).

Demonstrated skills in the following areas:
• Leadership, diplomatic skills, excellent interpersonal skills, and ability to bring together actors with diverse profiles and interests;
• Coordination of multidisciplinary expertise (including remote) and partnership facilitation;
• Skills in synthesizing and drafting technical and strategic documents (terms of reference, concept notes, methodologies, reports, etc.);
• Perfect command of French and English, both written and spoken;
• Proficiency in office tools, especially Excel, PPT, Word.

**2. Desired skills/expertise**
• Knowledge of the institutional civil protection ecosystem in the Mediterranean (Union for the Mediterranean, League of Arab States, UNDRR, etc.);
• Good knowledge of institutional capacity-building approaches and skills transfer;
• Strong ability to work in multicultural environments and to engage in dialogue with high-level institutional stakeholders;
• Proficiency in Arabic.

**3. Required Experience**
• At least 10 years of professional experience in civil protection/disaster risk management, including an operational position within a civil protection service;
• Proven experience in coordinating experts and technical partners, as well as in supervision and quality assurance of technical assistance services.

**4. Desired experience**
• Experience in implementing projects funded by international donors, notably the European Union;
• Professional experience in the Mediterranean region, particularly in Middle Eastern and North African (MENA) countries;
• Experience in cooperation with European and regional civil protection mechanisms, institutions, or networks.

## Additional information

The Technical Manager reports to the project manager. He/she will work closely with the project team deployed in Amman as well as with the Expertise France Headquarters teams based in Paris, notably through the support of the PRAR Thematic Cluster (Prevention, Reduction, and Adaptation to Risks).

The position is based in Amman, within the Expertise France office in Jordan, under a full-time expatriate contract.

The desired start date is January 2027.

The holder will be required to make regular trips to the partner countries of the project as well as to the Expertise France headquarters in France.

*Holding a valid HEAT (Hostile Environment Awareness Training) or equivalent certificate will be required for the position. If the candidate does not already have it, a dedicated training will be organized at the start of the project.*

To apply, please submit your CV and cover letter before 11/26/2026 inclusive.

**Application deadline: 29/11/2026 23:55**

## **The full Request for Proposals (RFP) document is available at the following link:** [**https://drive.google.com/drive/u/1/folders/1QvZCcVCehZfBnhyF9RJe6qvYdRoIJ6q6**](https://drive.google.com/drive/u/1/folders/1QvZCcVCehZfBnhyF9RJe6qvYdRoIJ6q6)

## **The Organisation**

CEFA (the European Committee for Training and Agriculture) is an Italian non-governmental organisation established in 1972 and active in 10 countries in Africa, Latin America and Eastern Europe. CEFA’s medium- and long-term interventions in the agricultural, environmental and human rights sectors are focused on the sustainable development of communities, achieved by involving local counterparts and by promoting the active participation of the beneficiaries.

## Overview of the Project

The GROW project (Gender-Responsive Opportunities for Water Access, Livelihood Recovery and Climate Resilience for Vulnerable Communities in West Pokot County, AID 012999/01/2) is an 18-month initiative funded by the Italian Agency for Development Cooperation (AICS) and implemented by CEFA, with PERUR - Rays of Hope as local partner, in West Pokot County, in the wards of Kodich, Kapchok and Kiwawa.

The project responds to humanitarian needs and promotes the resilience of vulnerable communities affected by recurrent climate shocks in arid and semi-arid areas, with particular attention to women, girls, children and persons with disabilities. Its integrated approach combines water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), agriculture and food security, livelihoods, gender equality, natural-resource management and disaster risk reduction.

Under this project, CEFA will construct 15 blocks of VIP latrines, for a total of 60 units, in nine (9) schools.

## Objectives of the Consultancy

The objective of the consultancy is to provide technical supervision of the works implemented by the two (2) selected contractors, including supervision during the Defects Liability Period (DLP), in order to ensure that the works are completed in accordance with the approved drawings, Bills of Quantities, technical specifications, contractual requirements, applicable quality standards and agreed timelines.

## Scope of the Consultancy

The assignment will be executed in two (2) phases:

- Phase 1: supervision of the construction works;
- Phase 2: supervision during the Defects Liability Period (DLP).

The project sites are located in Kodich, Kapchok and Kiwawa Wards of Pokot North Sub-County, West Pokot County.

## Specific Tasks per Phase

**Phase 1 - Supervision of the Construction Works**

In this phase the Consultant shall perform the principal services of the engineer responsible for the supervision of the works in accordance with the construction contract and FIDIC guidelines, which will include, but not be limited to:

1. Safeguard the interests of CEFA under the Works Contract, in any manner related to the Works Contract and the proper execution thereof;
2. Review and comment on or approve the Contractor's work programme, method statements, proposals for materials sources and quality management plan, including testing laboratories and procedures for testing;
3. Be on site at all times during the construction and be present at all key stages of the work (foundations, materials delivery, concreting, testing and commissioning, etc.) to observe the progress and quality of the Contractor's work;
4. Check and establish that the Contractor mobilises and supplies to the site all materials, equipment and machinery committed in its bid, and ensure that all such items remain on site until their release has been authorised. Ensure that the Contractor properly records all equipment, materials and labour supplied under the Contract;
5. Provide timely assistance and direction to the Contractor in all matters related to the interpretation or clarification of the Works Contract;
6. Issue instructions to the Contractor, in accordance with the authority specified in the Conditions of Contract, such that the works can proceed expeditiously;
7. Monitor progress against the programme, instructing the Contractor to revise its programme as necessary in order to meet the due completion dates;
8. Ensure that the works are executed in accordance with the drawings and that the quality of workmanship and materials complies with the technical specifications. Evaluate and determine the acceptability of substitute or "equal" materials and equipment proposed by the Contractor;
9. Liaise with communities and project-affected persons in relation to the works undertaken under the project; mediate any conflict or request between the Client, the Contractor, landowners or community members, and report to the Client in a timely manner;
10. Review and comment on, or recommend for approval, the Contractor's proposals for variations;
11. Prepare and submit reports on the progress of the works, the Contractor's performance, the quality of the works and the final reports after testing;
12. Review and certify all interim payment certificates submitted by the Contractor before forwarding them to CEFA;
13. Upon completion of the works, carry out the necessary inspection and propose to the CEFA team a date for a joint inspection;
14. Prepare the Completion Report, including verification of final quantities, outstanding or defective works, status of snag-list items and a recommendation to CEFA on provisional acceptance of the works and commencement of the Defects Liability Period. Where required, prepare the Certificate of Substantial Completion of Work for CEFA's approval and issuance;
15. Monitor the implementation of the Environmental and Social Management Plan (ESMP) through a checklist and ensure compliance with the environmental guidelines provided by CEFA;
16. Review and comment on or approve the as-built drawings prepared by the Contractor;
17. Review any documents shared by the Contractor;
18. Perform all other tasks not specifically mentioned above but which are necessary and essential to ensure the successful supervision and control of all construction activities, in accordance with the terms of the Works Contract.

**Phase 2 - Defects Liability Period**

During this phase the Consultant's tasks shall include the following:

1. Inspect the works jointly with CEFA and the Contractor once during the Contractor's Defects Liability Period and identify any defects, deficiencies, outstanding works or performance issues requiring corrective action;
2. Prepare a final deficiency list during the DLP where remedial work is required;
3. Supervise and verify all remedial works undertaken by the Contractor during the DLP and confirm whether the identified defects and deficiencies have been satisfactorily rectified in accordance with the contractual requirements;
4. Maintain and update the DLP deficiency / snag list until all outstanding items have been satisfactorily closed;
5. Upon expiry of the DLP, carry out the final inspection of the works jointly with CEFA and the Contractor and verify that all defects, deficiencies, outstanding works and snag-list items have been satisfactorily rectified;
6. Prepare the End of DLP Completion Report and recommend to CEFA the final acceptance of the works. Where required under the Works Contract, prepare the End of DLP Completion Certificate for CEFA's approval and issuance, and review and verify the Contractor's Final Statement of Accounts.

## Duration and Timeline

The duration of the contract is at least three (3) months for Phase 1 (supervision of the construction works), and six (6) months for Phase 2 (Defects Liability Period).

## **Staff Requirements and Qualifications**

The consulting firm must have qualified personnel with proven professional experience in the supervision of building and sanitation construction works. As proof of experience and membership of the respective professional bodies, the Consultant is required to attach personnel registration / membership certificates and detailed CVs. The consultant team shall have at least the following key personnel, with minimum qualifications and experience as follows:

**Team Leader / Resident Engineer (Civil Engineer)**

- Minimum of a bachelor's degree in Civil Engineering, Water Engineering or equivalent from a reputable institution (must provide academic certificate);
- Registered with the Engineers Board of Kenya (must provide EBK certificate).

The Team Leader will be the Resident Engineer responsible for the supervision of the works.

**Inspectors of Works (2 No.)**

- Minimum of a National Diploma or higher in Civil Engineering or equivalent from a reputable institution (must provide academic certificate);
- Registered with the Kenya Engineering Technology Registration Board (KETRB) and/or the National Construction Authority (NCA) and/or the Engineers Board of Kenya (EBK) (must provide registration certificate);
- Over three (3) years' progressive experience as inspector of works in similar building-related works.

**Titre du poste : Chargé(e) Protection et Cohésion Sociale**

**Supervisé par : Chief of Party**

**Responsable hiérarchique technique : Spécialiste Protection et Cohésion Sociale**

**Lieu d’affectation : Garoua-BoulaÏ, Région de l’Est, Cameroun**

**Zone d’intervention : Territoire du Cameroun**

**Période considérée : 12 mois (possibilité d’extension)**

**Type de contrat : Contrat national**

**Salaire & Avantages : Non-Management, band H**

**INTRODUCTION**

Le Conseil Danois pour les Réfugiés (DRC, pour Danish Refugee Council) porte assistance aux réfugiés et aux personnes déplacées à travers le monde. Nous fournissons une aide d’urgence à ces personnes, défendons leurs droits et renforçons leurs opportunités pour un meilleur avenir. Nous travaillons dans les zones affectées par les conflits, le long des routes de déplacement ainsi que dans les pays où les réfugiés s’établissent. En coopération avec les communautés locales, nous nous efforçons de trouver des solutions responsables et durables. Nous œuvrons en faveur d’une intégration réussie des communautés vulnérables et, lorsque cela est possible, à la réalisation de leur souhait de retourner chez elles.

Le Conseil Danois pour les Réfugiés (DRC) a été fondé au Danemark en 1956 et est depuis devenu une organisation humanitaire internationale comptant plus de 7 000 employés et 8 000 volontaires. Son siège est établi à Copenhague et l’organisation est présente dans 40 pays. DRC est une organisation non gouvernementale, à but non lucratif, politiquement neutre, indépendante et non confessionnelle.

Notre vision est celle d’une vie avec dignité pour toutes les personnes déplacées dans le monde. Tous nos efforts reposent sur notre boussole morale : l’humanité, le respect, l’indépendance, la neutralité, la participation, l’honnêteté et la transparence.

1. **CONTEXTE**

Dans le cadre de la mise en œuvre d'un projet transfrontalier entre le Cameroun et la République centrafricaine, financé par l'Union européenne et exécuté par un consortium regroupant plusieurs ONG internationales et nationales, le/la Chargé(e) de Protection et de Cohésion Sociale appuie le spécialiste Protection et Cohésion sociale dans l’accompagnement ALDEPA et APROSPEN pour la mise en œuvre des activités de protection et de cohésion sociale au Cameroun.

Le contexte transfrontalier de Garoua-Boulaï et des localités environnantes est marqué notamment par les mouvements de populations entre le Cameroun et la RCA, la présence de réfugiés, de personnes déplacées internes, de retournés et de communautés hôtes, ainsi que par des vulnérabilités socioéconomiques et des risques de tensions communautaires.

Dans ce contexte, le poste vise à contribuer à la prévention des violations des droits humains, à la réduction des risques de protection, à la promotion du vivre-ensemble, au renforcement des mécanismes communautaires de prévention et de gestion pacifique des conflits et à l'amélioration de l'accès des populations vulnérables aux services de protection.

**2. OBJET**

- Le/la Chargé(e) de Protection et de Cohésion Sociale a pour mission principale d’appuyer le/la Spécialiste Protection et Cohésion sociale dans l’appui technique aux partenaires ALDEPA et APROSPEN, qui ont la charge de la mise en œuvre des activités de Protection et de Cohésion sociale du SOLID2 au Cameroun.
- Le/la chargé(e) de Protection et de cohésion sociale sera dédiée au soutien aux activités de Protection au Cameroun, et représentera le/la Spécialiste Protection et Cohésion sociale lorsque celui/celle-ci sera en déplacement en RCA.

**3. RESPONSABILITES**

**Analyse des risques et évaluation des besoins de protection**

- Epauler le/la Spécialiste pour accompagner les partenaires dans la réalisation des analyses de protection suivant le cadre d’analyse de protection (PAF) afin d’identifier les risques, vulnérabilités, capacités et priorités de réponse dans les zones d’intervention.
- Epauler le/la Spécialiste pour accompagner les partenaires et les autorités locales pour conduire des analyses de conflits sensibles au genre et à l’âge afin d’identifier les facteurs de tensions intercommunautaires et transfrontalières.
- Epauler le/la Spécialiste pour l’évaluation de l’implication des enfants réfugiés et des communautés hôtes dans les activités minières afin d’en analyser les causes, les conséquences et les mesures de réponse

**Le/la chargé(e) de Protection et Cohésion sociale épaule le/la Spécialiste Protection et Cohésion sociale sur l’appui aux partenaires pour la mise en œuvre de l’ensemble des activités de protection**

- Accompagner les partenaires dans la conception et la mise en œuvre des activités de protection communautaire conformément aux standards internationaux.
- Appuyer les partenaires dans le renforcement des mécanismes communautaires d’identification, de prévention et de référencement des cas de protection.
- Soutenir les partenaires dans la facilitation de l’accès des personnes vulnérables aux services sociaux, sanitaires, psychosociaux, juridiques et administratifs.
- Accompagner les partenaires dans l’identification et le référencement sécurisé des personnes à besoins spécifiques vers les structures compétentes.
- Renforcer les capacités des partenaires pour la promotion de la protection contre l’exploitation et les abus sexuels (PEAS/PSEA).
- Veiller à ce que les partenaires appliquent les principes de confidentialité, de consentement éclairé, de dignité et de sécurité dans l’accompagnement des personnes concernées.
- Accompagner les partenaires dans la prévention et la réponse aux violences basées sur le genre (VBG), en coordination avec les spécialistes VBG.
- Soutenir les partenaires dans le renforcement et la mise en œuvre des mécanismes de protection de l’enfance, en collaboration avec les acteurs spécialisés.
- Appuyer les partenaires dans la promotion de l’accès à l’information sur les droits, les services disponibles et les mécanismes de recours.
- Accompagner les partenaires afin de garantir que toutes les activités mises en œuvre respectent le principe de « Ne pas nuire » (Do No Harm).

**Le/la chargé(e) de Protection et Cohésion sociale épaule le/la Spécialiste Protection et Cohésion sociale sur l’appui aux partenaires pour la mise en œuvre de l’ensemble des activités de promotion de la cohésion sociale et du vivre-ensemble**

- Avec les partenaires et en coordination étroite avec le/la Spécialiste Protection et Cohésion sociale, concevoir et mettre en œuvre des stratégies de renforcement de la cohésion sociale entre les communautés hôtes, réfugiées, déplacées et retournées ;
- Soutenir les partenaires dans l’organisation des dialogues communautaires et des espaces de concertation entre les différentes communautés ;
- Accompagner les partenaires dans le renforcement des comités communautaires de paix et de cohésion sociale ;
- Accompagner les partenaires dans le renforcement des mécanismes communautaires de prévention, de médiation et de résolution des conflits.
- Soutenir les partenaires dans la promotion de la participation inclusive des femmes, des jeunes et des leaders communautaires aux initiatives de paix et de cohésion sociale.
